Written to `ADR-001-notification-architecture.md` (79 lines).

**Recommendation: Redis Streams**, on a dedicated Redis instance, with a PostgreSQL outbox for ingestion and consumer-side idempotency keys for billing exactly-once. The decision rests on two verified facts that together neutralize Kafka's case: (1) Kafka's exactly-once semantics only hold when the output is written to Kafka itself — the design doc explicitly limits them for external systems (`design.md:205`), so billing emails/webhooks need consumer-side dedup with Kafka exactly as with Redis Streams; and (2) at your stated load (~2M tasks/month, 500 req/s peak; even 10x is low 10⁴ events/s), throughput is not the binding constraint — the 6-person team, zero Kafka experience, and 2-week budget are, and those all favor the technology you already run. The ADR documents the honest cons (weaker durability, no auto-sharding, memory-bounded retention) and explicit trigger conditions for revisiting Kafka.

All load-bearing claims were verified this session against the Kafka design doc, the Redis Streams docs, and `system_context.md` — line-cited inline. The one figure I could not verify (a throughput quote from Kafka's intro page) was removed rather than asserted.
